.TH CBMAP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_declare_scoped \- declare an ordered map handle that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_declare_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle with the name hm_name. It also declares the
companion type variables. Those variables carry key_t and val_t to every
later type-inferred macro. The macro also attaches the cleanup attribute. That
attribute destroys the map on every exit from the enclosing scope. The macro
creates nothing. The handle holds NULL until one of the init macros runs.

The macro differs from cbmap_declare(3) in exactly three ways. It
initializes the handle to NULL and does not leave it uninitialized. It also
initializes both companion type variables to NULL. And the handle carries
the cleanup attribute, so an exit from the scope destroys the map that the
handle names at that moment. The handle starts as NULL, and the library
ignores a NULL handle. A return before an init macro runs is therefore safe,
and nothing in the block reads an indeterminate object.

Use this macro with cbmap_init(3) or one of its variants when the
configuration of the map comes from inside the scope.
cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the one-step form. Use that form for a map
whose configuration is known at the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key. It also
         tells the init macro which order to give the tree
val_t    Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it. The library never
         gives it to the container
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
An explicit cbmap_destroy(3) before the end of the scope stays correct. It
sets the handle to NULL, and the cleanup at the exit of the scope then does
nothing
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that receives the handle therefore needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it. Do not store it in
a place that outlives the scope. Both actions give out a handle whose map
the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_declare_scoped(sorted, int, char *);
if (!load_config()) {
    return -1;             /* Nothing exists yet. Cleanup does nothing */
}
cbmap_init(sorted);
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
return 0;                  /* The cleanup destroys sorted here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_init (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_redeclare (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
